Ethics and Responsible AI

OcuScan AI is an educational Artificial Intelligence prototype designed to support students studying Optics and optical professionals in understanding visible ocular abnormalities through image analysis.

The project follows principles of responsible AI, transparency, privacy and human supervision.

1. Educational Purpose

OcuScan AI is intended exclusively for educational and informational purposes. It helps users explore ocular anatomy, recognize visible alterations and understand possible conditions through image-based learning.

2. No Medical Diagnosis

The system does not provide definitive medical diagnoses. It identifies visible characteristics and describes possible alterations that may be compatible with the uploaded image. AI-generated interpretations must not be considered clinical conclusions.

3. Human Responsibility

The assistant does not replace the professional judgment of an optometrist, optician or ophthalmologist. Optical professionals must not use its output as a substitute for clinical examinations or medical assessment. Suspected ocular conditions must be referred to an ophthalmologist when appropriate.

4. Patient Safety

The system must not recommend medication or treatments. If users report sudden vision loss, severe eye pain, chemical exposure or ocular trauma, they must be advised to seek urgent medical evaluation.

5. Image Limitations and Transparency

The quality of the analysis may be affected by image resolution, lighting, focus, viewing angle and the visibility of ocular structures. The assistant must distinguish objective observations from possible interpretations and communicate uncertainty whenever necessary.

6. Privacy and Data Protection

Users should upload only educational images or photographs shared with appropriate consent. Personal and identifiable patient information must not be entered. Image handling must respect applicable privacy and data protection requirements, including the GDPR.

7. Bias and Critical Thinking

AI-generated results may be incomplete or inaccurate. Students are encouraged to compare the explanations with reliable educational resources and develop independent critical thinking rather than relying exclusively on automated interpretations.

8. Responsible Use

OcuScan AI is designed to promote responsible use of Artificial Intelligence in optical education. Its purpose is to support learning, not to replace professional expertise, clinical responsibility or medical care.

OcuScan AI – Artificial Intelligence as a learning tool, with human knowledge and responsibility at the center.


